📋 The Full Sub-Branch to Main Workflow		
Step	                               Terminal Command	                                   What It Does (In Simple Terms)
1. Update Main	git checkout main git pull origin main	Switches you to the main branch and downloads the absolute latest code from GitHub so you don't start with outdated files.
2. Create Sub-Branch	git checkout  -b my-new -feature	           Creates a  Brand-new  Sub-branch called my-new-feature  And immediately switches you into it.
3. Write & Check Code (Make your file changes) git status	           You write your code in your code  editor. Running git status shows  You a list of the files you just changed.
4. Stage the Files	git add .	                                             Gathers all of your modified files and  Gets them ready to be locked into a save point.
5. Commit Locally   	git commit -m "Added a new feature"	Takes a permanent snapshot of your work  on your local computer with a descriptive  Message.
6. Push Sub-Branch	git push  origin my- New-feature	           Uploads your sub-branch to GitHub so  Your work is safely backed up in the cloud.
7. Switch to Main	git checkout main  Git pull origin main	Moves your cursor back onto the  Main branch so it can receive your new work.
8. Merge Sub-Branch	git merge my-new- Feature --no-edit	            Combines your sub-branch code into main.  The --no-edit flag skips the annoying Nano  Text editor.
9. Push to GitHub	git push origin main	                                  Sends the newly updated main branch  Up to GitHub. Your project is now fully updated!
10. Clean Up	           git branch -d my- New-feature	             Safely deletes the local sub-branch  Since its code is now safely inside main.


